Date: Thu, 28 May 2026 08:56:25 +0530
Subject: =?UTF-8?q?Blink:=20outerWidth/outerHeight=20overrides=20should=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?read=20availWidth/Height,=0Anot=20Width/Height?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

LocalDOMWindow::outerWidth() / outerHeight() were pulling their
Settings-side fingerprint overrides from GetScreenWidthOverride() /
GetScreenHeightOverride() — the screen-resource fields. That produced
window.outerWidth == screen.width and window.outerHeight ==
screen.height, which on real hardware is only possible when Chrome is
running borderless / true-fullscreen. On every normal desktop session
(taskbar present, window maximized but not fullscreen), real Chrome
returns outerWidth/Height == availWidth/Height — i.e. the screen size
minus the OS chrome (taskbar, menu bar). The equality
outerHeight == screen.height is therefore a strong bot-/automation
tell that anti-fingerprinting libraries (fpjs, creepjs, pixelscan,
incolumitas) actively check.

Switch both overrides to GetScreenAvailWidthOverride() and
GetScreenAvailHeightOverride() so a maximized FalconBrowser window
reports the same outerWidth/Height pattern as a maximized real
Chrome window. On setups with no side taskbar, availWidth ==
screen.width and there is no visible change; on Windows with a
bottom taskbar (the common case) availHeight == screen.height -
taskbar_px, which is what real Chrome reports.

The override comes from the fingerprint config the browser pushes
into Settings via the native UpdateFingerprintConfig path. That
config already populates both kScreen{Width,Height}Override and
kScreenAvail{Width,Height}Override based on the profile's claimed
display geometry, so this is a pure pick-the-right-field change — no
plumbing or config-shape work required.

Inline comments added at both call sites explain the reasoning so
that future refactors don't quietly switch back to the Width/Height
fields. The screen.width / screen.height getters (elsewhere in this
file) continue to read GetScreenWidth/HeightOverride and are
unaffected — those are still meant to report the full physical
resolution.

Verified manually: in a maximized FalconBrowser window on Windows
with a bottom taskbar, window.outerHeight now reports
screen.availHeight (1187), distinct from screen.height (1235),
matching real Chrome's behavior.

